#!/bin/sh
#
# Refuse a direct push to `platform`.
#
# ── Why this exists at all ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Branch protection needs a paid plan on a private repository and the API refuses to set it, so
# nothing on GitHub's side stops a push to the branch everyone else pulls. CI reports; it cannot
# block. This is the only guard that can be in the way at the moment it matters.
#
# It is not security. `--no-verify` goes straight past it and the message below says so. It is here
# so that pushing to `platform` is a *decision* rather than the thing that happens when somebody
# forgets to branch — which is a mistake worth catching, because one person deploys from their own
# working directory and an unreviewed commit on `platform` ships the next time they do.
#
# ── The message is the point ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A refusal that only says no leaves somebody with commits they are now frightened of. §7.7: errors
# say what to do next. So it prints the three lines that move the work onto a branch and put
# `platform` back, which is the whole recovery and loses nothing.

protected_ref="refs/heads/platform"

# stdin: <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>, one line per ref being pushed.
while read -r _local_ref _local_sha remote_ref _remote_sha; do
  [ "$remote_ref" = "$protected_ref" ] || continue

  cat >&2 <<'REFUSAL'

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Refusing to push straight to platform.

  Every change here goes through a pull request: the gate runs there, and one person
  deploys from their own working directory — so a commit that lands on platform
  without review is a commit that ships the next time they deploy.

  Your commits are safe. To move them onto a branch of their own:

      git switch -c short-name          # takes your commits with it
      git push -u origin short-name
      gh pr create --base platform

  Then put platform back where everyone else has it:

      git switch platform
      git reset --hard origin/platform

  CONTRIBUTING.md has the rest. If you genuinely mean to push to platform:

      git push --no-verify
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

REFUSAL

  exit 1
done

exit 0
